From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andres Cera <andres.cera@hotmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 09:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 22/29] media: rockchip: rkvenc: refuse register requests that clip a class

MOTIVATION. tests/rkvenc-invalid-ioctl.c --all-malformed failed 4
of 8 on a real Rock 5B+ against tests/expected-errno.tsv, so 0016
did not finish the job. A register request is SPLIT across every
class it overlaps and each part clamped to that class's range, so
bytes no class owns were dropped rather than refused: class-overrun
was accepted with rc=0 while most of it was silently discarded.
INIT_TRANS_TABLE bounded bytes but not alignment, so 2*N+1 still
divided to N whole entries and trans-table-odd-size was accepted,
leaving one u16 half written and trans_count claiming it whole.
The register-write copy_from_user() returned -EIO, so
bad-user-pointer reported an I/O error for an unreadable user
address. Reading the same paths found two more of the same shape
that no case covers: w_req_cnt/r_req_cnt accumulate across every
message in the ioctl and were unbounded, so two write messages each
spanning all nine classes wrote 18 parts into a 9-element array
inside the task; and rkvenc_extract_reg_offset_info() bounded
ELEMENTS while copying BYTES, so 8*128+7 bytes passed the count
check and overran elem[] by seven, a partial trailing element
included.

This patch has now been corrected TWICE by hardware, and the
correction is the interesting part. v1 required the summed
clamped parts to EQUAL the request's size, asserting that "a
request that lies wholly inside the classes it names" is what
librockchip-mpp sends. A cold-boot control encode on a real Rock
5B+ proved that FALSE: MPP's class BASE write is offset 0 size
96, reg_msg[] owns [0x0000,0x005c) = 92 bytes, so its last dword
lands in the 137-dword BASE-to-PIC hole and every production task
was refused, 0 bytes out against 1,854,524 from the same board
one RAUC slot earlier. v2 replaced equality with sum == span --
"the split consumed ONE contiguous run" -- which forgives a spill
off a class's edge but still refuses a span with a hole INSIDE
it.

H.265 sends exactly that shape, and v2 refused every H.265 encode
for it. MPP's HEVC register programme is ONE 3228-byte write over
SQI (488 bytes) and SCL (2716) with the genuine 24-byte SQI-to-
SCL map hole between them: 3204 covered against a 3228 span, so
"write request 00002000+3228 covers 3204 bytes, not one run" ->
"alloc task failed: -22" -> MPP's own "MPP_IOC_CFG_V1 failed ret
-1 errno 22". The request starts on SQI's first byte and ends on
SCL's last; it overruns nothing and drops nothing at either edge.
H.264 issues no multi-class write, so it never noticed -- which
is also why the H.264-only control encode v2 shipped behind did
not catch this.

BEHAVIOUR. req_coverage_check() asks WHERE the dropped bytes went
and WHETHER any class was left half-named. Three shapes reach it,
and only the third is a lie. ONE unbroken run, spilling off an
edge into the neighbouring hole -- MPP's 96-byte BASE write -- is
settled by sum == span alone, accepted and clamped exactly as it
was before 0022. SEVERAL runs where every run is a WHOLE class
and every gap is one of the map's own holes -- MPP's SQI+SCL
write -- is accepted by req_spans_whole_classes(), because
nothing was half-named: rkvenc_update_req() advances each part's
data pointer by the same amount it advances its offset, so every
byte the caller supplied for a real register still reaches that
register from the offset the caller put it at, and the only bytes
dropped are addresses no register answers to. SEVERAL runs that
CLIPPED a class to get there -- class-overrun, which starts one
dword inside BASE and runs to one dword inside ST -- is still
-EINVAL, and the clip is precisely what expected-errno.tsv calls
it: "starts inside a class and claims a size that runs past its
end". Contiguity and containment both miss a request running past
the LAST class, so the request is additionally bounded by the
map's own extent. Every bound is read out of reg_msg[], so a map
with different holes or none needs no change. READ and WRITE get
the SAME rule: both consume the CLAMPED per-class parts and
neither re-reads the caller's raw span, because rkvenc_result()
walks task->r_reqs[] and copies each part out of the class buffer
that owns it, bounded by 0016's rkvenc_class_reg_window(). Both
request counts are bounded by their own arrays. INIT_TRANS_TABLE
and SET_REG_ADDR_OFFSET both require a whole number of elements,
and the latter now bounds bytes against the room left rather than
elements against a count. Every copy_from_user() failure on this
path reports -EFAULT, matching the rule the expectation table
already states: EINVAL rejects the shape, EFAULT rejects the
buffer.

NON-GOALS. Does not change the per-class split itself, the clamp,
or 0016's shape and window checks. Does NOT tighten what the
clamp has always tolerated: a request may still spill into an
adjacent hole by as much as that hole holds, and those bytes are
still dropped silently. That is the pre-0022 contract, and memory
safety on this path is 0016's window check, not this one's --
this check refuses a misleading SHAPE. It does NOT give the read
side a weaker rule than the write side, even though only a write
was observed failing; the two paths are structurally identical
here, and an asymmetry with nothing behind it is how the next
false positive gets built. It does not object to a whole-class
span being LARGE -- a request naming BASE through ST end to end
is accepted, because it is honest about every byte it covers, and
size is not the property under test. Does not make the class
map's inclusive/half-open mismatch between req_over_class() and
rkvenc_result() consistent, and does not widen reg_msg[]'s BASE
end to the 24 dwords MPP actually writes; both are pre-existing,
separate, and not changeable without a board. Does NOT fix the
valid-after-failures case, which fails for an unrelated reason
recorded in docs/UPSTREAM-STATUS.md.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix completing 0016, which is
itself a fix to the UAPI parser 0001 imports. No upstream
counterpart exists.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Every version of this patch was corrected by a
REAL Rock 5B+, this one included. The H.265 refusal was root-
caused from the board's own dmesg, and the fix was then built as
a module against the running 7.1.7-ceralive-rk3588 and hot-
swapped onto it: mpph265enc, which had produced 0 bytes at every
geometry, produced decodable HEVC afterwards, and the H.264
control encode was unchanged. rkvenc-invalid-ioctl
--all-malformed was re-run on the same module and class-overrun,
trans-table-odd-size and bad-user-pointer stayed rejected. No
test expectation changes: expected-errno.tsv already demanded
these values. The general lesson stands and is now doubly
earned -- a control encode belongs in EVERY rkvenc UAPI change's
acceptance, and it has to cover every codec the board can be
asked for, because v2 passed an H.264-only one. See
docs/UPSTREAM-STATUS.md and docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.2, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0022-rkvenc-ioctl-request-coverage-and-element-bounds.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -226,6 +226,149 @@ static int rkvenc_class_reg_window(struct rkvenc_task *task, u32 class,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * What the per-class split of ONE request consumed, and where.
+ *
+ * `bytes` is 0 exactly when no class matched; `lo` starts at U32_MAX so the
+ * first part sets it, and `hi` is one past the last covered byte.
+ */
+struct req_coverage {
+	u32 bytes;
+	u32 lo;
+	u32 hi;
+};
+
+static void req_coverage_add(struct req_coverage *cov,
+			     const struct mpp_request *part)
+{
+	cov->lo = min(cov->lo, part->offset);
+	cov->hi = max(cov->hi, part->offset + part->size);
+	cov->bytes += part->size;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Is the covered span exactly a run of WHOLE register classes, separated only
+ * by the map's own unowned holes?
+ *
+ * Every class the span touches must lie ENTIRELY inside it. A class the span
+ * merely CLIPS is a class the request half-named -- it "starts inside a class
+ * and claims a size that runs past its end", which is `class-overrun`'s own
+ * description in tests/expected-errno.tsv. With nothing clipped, the classes
+ * the span contains ARE the bytes the split kept, so owned == cov->bytes
+ * proves the remainder of the span is unowned map territory rather than a
+ * class that went missing along the way.
+ */
+static bool req_spans_whole_classes(const struct rkvenc_hw_info *hw,
+				    const struct req_coverage *cov)
+{
+	u32 owned = 0;
+	u32 i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < hw->reg_class; i++) {
+		u32 s = hw->reg_msg[i].base_s;
+		u32 e = hw->reg_msg[i].base_e + (u32)sizeof(u32);
+
+		/* Disjoint from the span, so it has no opinion about it. */
+		if (e <= cov->lo || s >= cov->hi)
+			continue;
+		/* Touched but not contained: the span clipped this class. */
+		if (s < cov->lo || e > cov->hi)
+			return false;
+		owned += e - s;
+	}
+
+	return owned == cov->bytes;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Decide whether the per-class split may stand in for the request userspace
+ * actually asked for.
+ *
+ * A register request is SPLIT across every class it overlaps and each part
+ * CLAMPED to that class's range, so bytes no class owns are dropped rather
+ * than refused. reg_msg[] is a SPARSE map: no two classes abut -- every
+ * neighbouring pair is separated by a hole, from six dwords (SQI to SCL) to
+ * 770 (PIC to RC) -- and it stops dead at DBG's end. So the dropped bytes come
+ * in three shapes, and only the last of them is a lie:
+ *
+ *   - the split covered ONE unbroken run and the request merely spilled off
+ *     its edge into the neighbouring hole. The clamp has always absorbed that
+ *     silently, and it is what librockchip-mpp's class BASE write does: 96
+ *     bytes where the map owns 92 ([0x0000,0x005c)), so the last dword falls
+ *     in the BASE-to-PIC hole and is dropped. Harmless, and the only
+ *     behaviour this hardware has ever had.
+ *
+ *   - the split covered SEVERAL runs, but every run is a WHOLE class and
+ *     every gap between them is one of the map's own holes. Nothing was
+ *     half-named: each byte the caller supplied for a real register still
+ *     reaches that register, from the offset the caller put it at, because
+ *     rkvenc_update_req() advances the per-part data pointer by the same
+ *     amount it advances the offset. MPP's H.265 register programme is
+ *     exactly this shape -- ONE 3228-byte write spanning SQI (488 bytes) and
+ *     SCL (2716) with the 24-byte SQI-to-SCL hole between them -- and
+ *     refusing it refused every H.265 encode this driver has ever been asked
+ *     for, while H.264, which issues no such write, went on working.
+ *
+ *   - the split covered several runs and CLIPPED a class to do it, so the
+ *     driver stitched PARTIAL pieces of the register file together and
+ *     reported the whole span as programmed -- or, worse, as read back. That
+ *     is `class-overrun` in tests/expected-errno.tsv, and it is the shape
+ *     worth refusing.
+ *
+ * The discriminator is therefore not how MANY bytes were dropped, and not
+ * merely whether the run is unbroken, but whether every byte the split
+ * dropped INSIDE the span is unowned map territory and no class was left
+ * half-named. The parts are disjoint subranges, so sum == span still settles
+ * the one-run case without touching reg_msg[]; only a broken span has to ask
+ * the map whether its gaps are real. Bounding the request by the map's own
+ * extent then stops it claiming space past the last class, which neither test
+ * can catch because there is no following class to notice. Every bound is
+ * read out of reg_msg[], so a map with different holes -- or none -- needs no
+ * change here.
+ *
+ * READ and WRITE get the SAME rule, deliberately. Both consume the CLAMPED
+ * per-class parts and neither ever touches the caller's raw span again:
+ * rkvenc_extract_task_msg() copies each part into the class buffer that owns
+ * it, and rkvenc_result() walks task->r_reqs[] -- the parts, not the original
+ * request -- copying each one back out of its own class buffer, both bounded
+ * by 0016's rkvenc_class_reg_window(). A multi-class read is therefore no
+ * more exposed than a multi-class write, so weakening this test on one side
+ * only would be an asymmetry with nothing behind it.
+ */
+static int req_coverage_check(struct rkvenc_task *task,
+			      const struct mpp_request *req,
+			      const struct req_coverage *cov)
+{
+	const struct rkvenc_hw_info *hw = task->hw_info;
+	u32 map_s = U32_MAX;
+	u32 map_e = 0;
+	u32 i;
+
+	/* Nothing in the map owns any of it. */
+	if (!cov->bytes)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	/*
+	 * A hole INSIDE the covered span is disjoint runs stitched together --
+	 * unless every run is a whole class and every hole is the map's own.
+	 */
+	if (cov->bytes != cov->hi - cov->lo &&
+	    !req_spans_whole_classes(hw, cov))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < hw->reg_class; i++) {
+		map_s = min(map_s, hw->reg_msg[i].base_s);
+		map_e = max(map_e, hw->reg_msg[i].base_e + (u32)sizeof(u32));
+	}
+	/*
+	 * A class matched, so req_shape_valid() has already ruled out the wrap
+	 * this addition would otherwise have to guard against.
+	 */
+	if (req->offset < map_s || req->offset + req->size > map_e)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 /* ---- Check if request overlaps a class ---- */
 static bool req_over_class(struct mpp_request *req,
 			   struct rkvenc_task *task, u32 class)
@@ -296,8 +439,9 @@ static int rkvenc2_extract_rcb_info(struct rkvenc2_rcb_info *rcb_inf,
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 	if (copy_from_user(rcb_inf->elem, req->data, req->size)) {
+		/* Bad buffer, not a bad shape: EFAULT, as everywhere else. */
 		rkvenc_err("copy_from_user failed\n");
-		return -EINVAL;
+		return -EFAULT;
 	}
 	rcb_inf->cnt = cnt;
 
@@ -322,12 +466,28 @@ static int rkvenc_extract_task_msg(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 		switch (req->cmd) {
 		case MPP_CMD_SET_REG_WRITE: {
 			void *data;
+			struct req_coverage cov = { .lo = U32_MAX };
 			struct mpp_request *wreq;
 
 			for (j = 0; j < hw->reg_class; j++) {
 				if (!req_over_class(req, task, j))
 					continue;
 
+				/*
+				 * w_reqs[] holds one clamped part per class, so
+				 * RKVENC_CLASS_BUTT is its natural maximum --
+				 * but w_req_cnt accumulates across EVERY message
+				 * in the ioctl, and nothing bounded it. Two
+				 * write messages each spanning every class
+				 * therefore wrote 18 parts into a 9-element
+				 * array inside the kzalloc'd task.
+				 */
+				if (task->w_req_cnt >= ARRAY_SIZE(task->w_reqs)) {
+					rkvenc_err("more than %zu write requests in one task\n",
+						   ARRAY_SIZE(task->w_reqs));
+					ret = -EINVAL;
+					goto fail;
+				}
 				ret = rkvenc_alloc_class_msg(task, j);
 				if (ret) {
 					rkvenc_err("alloc class msg %d fail.\n", j);
@@ -353,21 +513,41 @@ static int rkvenc_extract_task_msg(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 					goto fail;
 				}
 				if (copy_from_user(data, wreq->data, wreq->size)) {
+					/*
+					 * An unreadable user buffer is EFAULT.
+					 * -EIO said the hardware had an I/O
+					 * problem, which is a different claim
+					 * about a different component.
+					 */
 					rkvenc_err("copy_from_user fail, offset %08x\n", wreq->offset);
-					ret = -EIO;
+					ret = -EFAULT;
 					goto fail;
 				}
+				req_coverage_add(&cov, wreq);
 				task->reg[j].valid = 1;
 				task->w_req_cnt++;
 			}
+			ret = req_coverage_check(task, req, &cov);
+			if (ret) {
+				rkvenc_err("write request %08x+%u covers %u bytes, not one run nor whole classes\n",
+					   req->offset, req->size, cov.bytes);
+				goto fail;
+			}
 		} break;
 		case MPP_CMD_SET_REG_READ: {
+			struct req_coverage cov = { .lo = U32_MAX };
 			struct mpp_request *rreq;
 
 			for (j = 0; j < hw->reg_class; j++) {
 				if (!req_over_class(req, task, j))
 					continue;
 
+				if (task->r_req_cnt >= ARRAY_SIZE(task->r_reqs)) {
+					rkvenc_err("more than %zu read requests in one task\n",
+						   ARRAY_SIZE(task->r_reqs));
+					ret = -EINVAL;
+					goto fail;
+				}
 				ret = rkvenc_alloc_class_msg(task, j);
 				if (ret) {
 					rkvenc_err("alloc class msg reg %d fail.\n", j);
@@ -379,9 +559,23 @@ static int rkvenc_extract_task_msg(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 					rkvenc_err("bad read request for class %d\n", j);
 					goto fail;
 				}
+				req_coverage_add(&cov, rreq);
 				task->reg[j].valid = 1;
 				task->r_req_cnt++;
 			}
+			/*
+			 * The read side is where an unowned byte does real
+			 * damage: rkvenc_result() copies each part back to
+			 * userspace, so a request stitched across the map's
+			 * holes used to be clamped here and then reported as
+			 * though the whole of it had been read.
+			 */
+			ret = req_coverage_check(task, req, &cov);
+			if (ret) {
+				rkvenc_err("read request %08x+%u covers %u bytes, not one run nor whole classes\n",
+					   req->offset, req->size, cov.bytes);
+				goto fail;
+			}
 		} break;
 		case MPP_CMD_SET_REG_ADDR_OFFSET: {
 			/*
@@ -960,8 +1154,17 @@ static int rkvenc_process_request(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 		 * rounded cnt down, passed this check and then overran
 		 * trans_table by one byte. Bound the bytes, which is what the
 		 * copy actually consumes.
+		 *
+		 * Bounding the bytes was necessary and NOT sufficient: 2*N+1
+		 * bytes still divides to N whole entries, so an odd size well
+		 * inside sizeof(trans_table) passed both halves, and the copy
+		 * left the last u16 half written while trans_count claimed it
+		 * whole. rkvenc_translate_reg_address() then read that entry as
+		 * a register index. A table of u16 is only ever a whole number
+		 * of u16, so require it.
 		 */
-		if (cnt <= 0 || req->size > sizeof(session->trans_table)) {
+		if (cnt <= 0 || req->size % sizeof(u16) ||
+		    req->size > sizeof(session->trans_table)) {
 			rkvenc_err("trans table size %u (%d entries) out of range\n",
 				   req->size, cnt);
 			return -EINVAL;
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_task.c
@@ -283,17 +283,37 @@ int rkvenc_translate_reg_address(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 int rkvenc_extract_reg_offset_info(struct reg_offset_info *off_inf,
 				   struct mpp_request *req)
 {
-	int max_size = ARRAY_SIZE(off_inf->elem);
-	int cnt = req->size / sizeof(off_inf->elem[0]);
+	u32 elem_size = sizeof(off_inf->elem[0]);
+	u32 max_size = ARRAY_SIZE(off_inf->elem);
+	u32 cnt = req->size / elem_size;
 
-	if ((cnt + off_inf->cnt) > max_size) {
-		rkvenc_err("count %d, total %d, max_size %d\n",
+	/*
+	 * The bound counted ELEMENTS while the copy consumed BYTES -- the same
+	 * mismatch INIT_TRANS_TABLE had, and it is worse here because the
+	 * element is eight bytes wide: elem_size * max_size + 7 bytes still
+	 * divides to max_size whole elements, so the count bound was satisfied
+	 * and the copy then ran seven bytes past the end of elem[]. A partial
+	 * trailing element was accepted too, and counted as whole, so the last
+	 * entry rkvenc_query_reg_offset_info() searches was half userspace and
+	 * half whatever the allocation held.
+	 *
+	 * Require a whole number of elements, then bound the BYTES against the
+	 * room actually left -- which is what copy_from_user() consumes.
+	 */
+	if (!req->size || req->size % elem_size) {
+		rkvenc_err("offset info size %u is not a whole number of elements\n",
+			   req->size);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+	if (off_inf->cnt > max_size ||
+	    req->size > (max_size - off_inf->cnt) * elem_size) {
+		rkvenc_err("count %u, total %u, max_size %u\n",
 			   cnt, off_inf->cnt, max_size);
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 	if (copy_from_user(&off_inf->elem[off_inf->cnt], req->data, req->size)) {
 		rkvenc_err("copy_from_user failed\n");
-		return -EINVAL;
+		return -EFAULT;
 	}
 	off_inf->cnt += cnt;
 
